      "writtenOn": "2023-10-03T07:55:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In general, for AES-GCM, it is advisable to utilize a 12-byte nonce, as recommended in both the referenced paper [1] and the bssl specification [2]. The purpose of the nonce is to set up the 16-byte IV. The first 12 bytes of the IV are populated by the nonce, while the remaining 4 bytes represent the GCM counter. When a nonce _with other lengths are effectively randomized, which means one must consider collisions_ [3].\n\n[1]: NIST SP 800-38D: https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/legacy/sp/nistspecialpublication800-38d.pdf \u003e 5.2.1.1 Input data\n[2] https://commondatastorage.googleapis.com/chromium-boringssl-docs/aead.h.html#EVP_aead_aes_256_gcm\n[3] http://google3/third_party/openssl/boringssl/src/crypto/fipsmodule/modes/gcm.c;l\u003d258;rcl\u003d570057516",
      "parentUuid": "8d5ce3f7_043ed924",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 41,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 43
      },
      "revId": "c34e0b47080dc684a6b24ad4ddd485ba3d77fcd6",
