    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f2f4834d_11fc39e4",
        "filename": "rialto/src/requests/keyblob.rs",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1958315
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-10-03T07:55:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is fine since the security of GCM primarily relies on the uniqueness of the IV/key combination[1]. By ensuring that a unique key is used each time, the uniqueness of the IV/key combination is already guaranteed, and the probability of encountering the same IV/key is no greater than 2^-32. However, given the significance of these factors, it also wouldn\u0027t hurt to incorporate a random nonce as an added precaution. What are your thoughts on this? @drysdale@google.com\n\nI\u0027ll merge this cl into the previous one and ask a cryptographer to take a look.\n\n[1] [NIST SP 800-38D](https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/legacy/sp/nistspecialpublication800-38d.pdf) \u003e 8 Uniqueness Requirement on IVs and Keys",
      "parentUuid": "f0dd006f_cc6ee5fa",
